.TH SBX 1 "" "sbx 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sbx \- run a program in a bubblewrap sandbox
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sbx
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIPROGRAM\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.br
.B sbx preview
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIPROGRAM\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.br
.B sbx show
[\fIoptions\fR] \fINAME\fR
.br
.B sbx doctor
.br
.B sbx list
[\fB\-\-all\fR]
.br
.B sbx rebind
[\fB\-\-dismiss\fR] \fINAME\fR
.br
.B sbx stop
\fINAME\fR
.br
.B sbx clean
[\fB\-\-yes\fR] \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-unused\fR
.br
.B sbx reset
[\fB\-\-yes\fR] \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-default\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR
.br
.B sbx link
\fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-remove\fR \fINAME\fR
.br
.B sbx open
\fIFILE\fR
.br
.B sbx desktop
\fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-remove\fR \fINAME\fR | \fB\-\-install\-opener\fR | \fB\-\-remove\-opener\fR |
\fB\-\-install\-menu\fR | \fB\-\-remove\-menu\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sbx
starts a program inside a
.BR bwrap (1)
sandbox. The program sees the system read\-only, an empty private home instead of yours, a fresh
.I /tmp
and
.IR /run ,
a cleared environment and a blocklist of system calls. What it may see beyond that is set with options and
settings files. Options go before the program name.
.PP
The program starts in its private home,
.IR ~/.sbx\-homes/NAME ,
where NAME is the program's name (or the value of
.BR \-\-name ).
Use full paths, or
.B \-\-here
to share the current folder. If an argument names something that exists on your side but is not visible
inside, sbx says so.
.PP
A program named like a command is run with
.BR "sbx \-\- clean" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B sbx [options] PROGRAM [args]
Run the program in the sandbox. Its exit status is the program's (128 plus the signal number if a signal
ended it).
.TP
.B sbx preview [options] PROGRAM [args]
Print the bwrap command instead of running it.
.TP
.B sbx show [options] NAME
Print the settings that apply to a program and which file or flag each one came from, where the program comes from, and a decision waiting about it.
.TP
.B sbx doctor
Check bubblewrap, the helpers (xdg\-dbus\-proxy, notify\-send, systemd\-run), Wayland support and the
settings files.
.TP
.B sbx list
List the programs you set up (a settings file, an sbx link or launcher, or running now) and say which are running, where each comes from (System, Downloaded, Home, link, menu, Missing, moved from) and whether a decision about it is waiting. Every
.B sbx PROGRAM
run leaves a private home behind, so a program you only ever started from the terminal is left out;
.B \-\-all
lists those too, after the others, System programs first and Downloaded ones last.
.TP
.B sbx rebind NAME
Accept the decision that is waiting for NAME: the file that now runs under that name is the same program as before, so the name belongs to it from now on, with the settings and private home it had. Errors when nothing is waiting, or when the situation has changed since (see
.BR "MOVED PROGRAMS" ).
.B sbx rebind \-\-dismiss NAME
drops the decision and changes nothing else; sbx asks again the next time the program starts from there.
.TP
.B sbx stop NAME
Ask a running program to stop (SIGTERM).
.TP
.B sbx clean [\-\-yes] NAME
Delete a program's private home and its machine\-id. Refused while the program is running.
.B sbx clean \-\-unused
deletes the private homes of every program nobody set up (no settings file, link or launcher, not running) that holds under 64 KiB, after listing them and asking, unless \-\-yes is given.
.TP
.B sbx reset [\-\-yes] NAME | \-\-default | \-\-all
Delete settings files so the built\-in defaults apply again: the program's own file (NAME), the default settings
(\-\-default), or every settings file (\-\-all). Lists the files and asks first, unless \-\-yes is given. Private homes,
machine\-ids and menu launchers are not touched; see
.B sbx clean
and
.BR "sbx desktop \-\-remove" .
.TP
.B sbx link NAME [\-\-desktop] | \-\-remove NAME [\-\-desktop]
Make typing NAME in a terminal start the program through sbx, like firejail's firecfg: a link
.I ~/.local/bin/NAME
to sbx, which runs the real program of that name from your PATH. Needs
.I ~/.local/bin
before the program's own folder in PATH (sbx warns if not). The program's arguments are its own, not sbx options; its
settings are the ones for NAME. Starting the program by its full path still skips sbx. Inside a sandbox the link starts the
program directly.
.B \-\-desktop
also does what
.B sbx desktop
does for every app\-menu launcher whose Exec line starts NAME; with
.B \-\-remove
it takes those out again. Without it, sbx only says when such a launcher exists.
.TP
.B sbx open FILE
Run a downloaded program with sensible defaults. This is what "Open with" calls; see
.BR "DOWNLOADED PROGRAMS" .
.TP
.B sbx desktop NAME
Make a launcher (a
.I .desktop
file) start the program through sbx. NAME is the launcher's id (firefox, org.mozilla.firefox) or, when no launcher has that id, the program the launcher starts (google\-chrome\-stable). A launcher in
.I ~/.local/share/applications
that sbx did not make is never replaced.
.B \-\-remove
undoes that.
.TP
.B sbx desktop \-\-install\-opener
Add "Run in sbx" to the file manager's Open With.
.B \-\-remove\-opener
undoes it.
.TP
.B sbx desktop \-\-install\-menu
Add sbx\-gui to your app menu.
.B \-\-remove\-menu
takes it out again. It needs sbx\-gui installed as a root\-owned file in
.IR /usr/local/bin :
only such a copy is ever started from the menu, so a program running as you cannot replace it.
.TP
.B sbx schema
Print the switches and limits that exist, as JSON. sbx\-gui reads this.
.SH OPTIONS
Most options also exist as a setting (for example
.B \-\-net=none
is
.BR net=0 ).
Flags can only change a setting in the direction shown here; there is no flag to turn the network back on.
.B \-\-ignore\-config
starts from the built\-in defaults.
.TP
.B \-\-net=none
No network. This and
.B \-\-net\-private
also close abstract Unix sockets.
.TP
.B \-\-net\-private
Keep this computer private: with the network on, the program gets its own network through
.BR pasta (1)
that reaches the internet but not
localhost or the services running on this computer. Your local network stays reachable, so this is not a firewall.
/etc/resolv.conf is replaced by a generated one, and /sys/class/net is hidden. Needs
.BR pasta ;
sbx does not start without it. Does nothing, with a warning, when the network is off.
Setting:
.BR net_private=1 .
.TP
.B \-\-private
The private home is erased on exit.
.TP
.B \-\-here
Share the current folder, read\-write. Not your whole home and not hidden folders.
.TP
.BI \-\-whitelist= PATH
Share a file or folder, read\-write. Can be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-read\-only= PATH
Share a file or folder, read\-only. Can be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-sensitive
Allow sharing ~/.ssh, ~/.gnupg, ~/.aws, browser profiles and other secret folders. They are refused otherwise.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wayland
Hide the Wayland socket.
.TP
.B \-\-wayland\-strict
No Wayland at all if the compositor cannot restrict it.
.TP
.B \-\-x11
Share the X11 socket. Weaker isolation.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-gpu
Hide /dev/dri and the NVIDIA devices.
.TP
.B \-\-gpu\-render\-only
Of /dev/dri, share only the render nodes (renderD*) that programs draw with, not the card* nodes that can also
drive the display. The NVIDIA devices are not affected.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-sound
Hide PipeWire and PulseAudio, which includes the microphone.
.TP
.BR \-\-camera ", " \-\-alsa ", " \-\-input ", " \-\-kvm ", " \-\-rocm ", " \-\-printing
Share /dev/video* (and the Camera portal), /dev/snd, /dev/input (and /run/udev, so controllers are
recognised), /dev/kvm, /dev/kfd, or the CUPS socket.
.TP
.B \-\-tray
Let the program show a tray icon and answer media keys, through xdg\-dbus\-proxy. Works without
.BR \-\-portals ;
only the tray and media\-key names are allowed on the bus.
.TP
.B \-\-ssh\-agent
Share your SSH agent socket. The program can sign as you while it runs.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-theme
Do not show your fonts, icons and GTK and fontconfig settings, and do not pass on your title\-bar button layout
and cursor theme and size. Without this option sbx reads those from your desktop settings (dconf, which the
sandbox cannot reach) and gives the program only them, as a small GSettings key file, so that for example
GNOME's minimize and maximize buttons appear.
.TP
.B \-\-compat
Pass through the graphics and toolkit variables that are set (WGPU_*, ICED_BACKEND, MESA_*, RADV_*,
VK_ICD_FILENAMES, SDL_VIDEODRIVER, QT_QPA_PLATFORM, GDK_BACKEND and similar). Tokens, keys and everything else
stay out.
.TP
.B \-\-portals
Allow notifications and the basic desktop portals (open links, file chooser, print, settings, trash, e\-mail and a
few more) through xdg\-dbus\-proxy.
.TP
.BR \-\-portal\- \fIWHAT\fR
One more desktop portal each, WHAT being screenshot, screencast, location, shortcuts, wallpaper, launcher,
remote\-desktop, input\-capture or secret (settings: portal_screenshot=1 and so on). They need
.BR \-\-portals .
.TP
.B \-\-hostname
The program sees the hostname "sbx", also in /etc/hostname and /etc/hosts.
.TP
.B \-\-privacy
The program sees a minimal /etc/passwd: your user name, root and nobody. Your full name (the GECOS field) and the other
accounts on this computer are not in it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-userns
The program cannot create user namespaces. Programs that build their own sandbox (Chromium, Electron, Flatpak,
nested bubblewrap) need them.
.TP
.BI \-\-env= NAME
Pass a variable through.
.TP
.BI \-\-setenv= NAME = VALUE
Set a variable. HOME, USER, LOGNAME, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and SBX_ACTIVE are protected.
.TP
.BI \-\-allow\-syscall= NAME
Turn one of the extra blocked system calls back on: io_uring, userfaultfd, process_vm_readv, process_vm_writev,
kcmp, pidfd_getfd, open_tree, move_mount, fsopen, fsconfig, fsmount, fspick, mount_setattr.
.TP
.BR \-\-memory=2G ", " \-\-tasks=512 ", " \-\-cpu=200%
Resource limits, through
.BR "systemd\-run \-\-user" .
.TP
.BI \-\-name= NAME
Use another private home, machine\-id and settings name than the program's.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-config
Ignore the settings files for this run.
.TP
.B \-\-rebind
Accept that this file is the same program as the one that first used its name, without asking. Only needed when sbx cannot tell by itself; see
.BR "MOVED PROGRAMS" .
.PP
Shared paths must be inside your home (not the home itself), on /mnt, /media or /run/media, or in /tmp.
~/.config/sbx and ~/.sbx\-homes can never be shared. A folder is mounted before what is inside it, so a read\-write
share inside a read\-only one stays writable.
.SH SETTINGS
Plain
.IB key = value
files; # starts a comment.
.I ~/.config/sbx/default.conf
applies to every program;
.I ~/.config/sbx/apps/NAME.conf
applies to one program and overrides the default. For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
net=0
gpu=0
portals=1
whitelist=~/Projects
read_only=~/.gitconfig
env=HTTPS_PROXY
setenv=GTK_THEME=Adwaita:dark
memory=2G
.fi
.RE
.PP
Switches take 0 or 1. Defaults: net=1, net_private=0, wayland=1, wayland_strict=0, x11=0, gpu=1, gpu_render_only=0, sound=1, portals=0, private=0,
hostname=0, privacy=0, userns=1, theme=1, camera=0, alsa=0, input=0, kvm=0, rocm=0, printing=0, ssh_agent=0,
allow_sensitive=0, compat=0, tray=0, and the extra portals portal_screenshot, portal_screencast, portal_location,
portal_shortcuts, portal_wallpaper, portal_launcher, portal_remote_desktop, portal_input_capture and
portal_secret (all 0, and only used when portals=1). Anything else, such as net=maybe, is reported with its file
and line number and ignored.
.PP
Lists that add up across the two files: whitelist, read_only, env, setenv, allow_syscall. Limits: memory, tasks,
cpu; an empty value in a program's file cancels the default's. Command\-line flags beat the files.
.PP
.B sbx show
prints the result of all this.
.BR sbx\-gui
edits the same files: run
.BR sbx\-gui ,
.B sbx\-gui NAME
or
.BR "sbx\-gui \-\-default" .
.SH DOWNLOADED PROGRAMS
After
.B sbx desktop \-\-install\-opener
a program file can be started from the file manager with Open With, Run in sbx, or from a shell with
.BR "sbx open FILE" .
A program with no settings file of its own starts with these defaults: its own folder, read\-write, and it starts
there (generic folders such as your home, Downloads, Desktop and Documents, and hidden folders, are never shared
this way); game controllers (/dev/input); and the usual network, GPU, sound and Wayland. It runs even without the
execute bit if it is a dynamically linked ELF program: the bit is set only while it runs and then put back. If a
settings file exists for that name, it is used exactly as written and none of these defaults apply.
.PP
A file that has the execute bit still starts directly when double\-clicked; sbx cannot intercept that. These
defaults trade isolation for convenience; for something you do not trust at all use
.BR "sbx \-\-net=none \-\-private FILE" .
.SH MOVED PROGRAMS
A program name owns its settings and private home. sbx records which file first ran under it (the path, the size, the modification time and the SHA\-256 of the file) and, when the name runs from another path, decides by the first of these that matches:
.TP
.B a
The file has the same content: the same program, moved or copied. Accepted silently; the record follows it.
.TP
.B b
Other content, the old path is gone, the same file name: moved, then updated. Accepted, with one line saying so.
.TP
.B c
Other content, the old path is gone, another file name: unclear. On a terminal sbx asks; without one the decision is left pending.
.TP
.B d
Other content, the old path still exists: two programs want one name. Refused and left pending; use
.B \-\-name=OTHER
for the other one.
.PP
A program that writes beside its own file finds that in its private home, under the folder the file is in (~/Downloads/settings.json is ~/.sbx\-homes/NAME/Downloads/settings.json). When a move is accepted and the old file is gone, sbx moves what is stored under the old folder to the new folder, inside the private home only, and says so in one line. It never overwrites, never follows a link, and does nothing for a copy or for a file outside your home.
.PP
Moves between system folders (/usr, /opt, /snap, ...) are always fine. A run from the recorded path is never held up by the file's content: a changed size or modification time only updates the record. Rule b means that once the original is deleted, a file with the same file name can take over the name; the sandbox still applies to it, with that program's settings.
.PP
Pending means the program was not started. sbx writes
.IR ~/.config/sbx/pending/NAME ,
sends a notification (with an "Open sbx" button where notify\-send supports actions) and exits with an error. Decide with
.B sbx rebind NAME
or
.BR "sbx rebind \-\-dismiss NAME" ;
sbx\-gui has a button for each. A decision that no longer applies, for example because the file is back at its recorded path, is dropped.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.B sbx doctor
checks that everything sbx needs works on this machine. Every run is also logged to
.IR ~/.local/state/sbx/sbx.log
(readable only by you, trimmed when it passes 1 MB): the start, the switches that were on, warnings, errors and
the exit code. When sbx has no terminal (started from the app menu, a file manager or sbx\-gui), what the program
prints to stderr goes into the log too, without control characters and at most 2 MB per run. A program that
exits with an error within 10 seconds also raises a desktop notification, if
.BR notify\-send (1)
is installed; where notifications support buttons, clicking it runs
.BR "sbx\-gui \-\-log NAME" .
That page, and the terminal button in sbx\-gui's header, show the checks of
.B sbx doctor
and the recent log.
For a program whose last run failed with a known error, the page also suggests the setting that usually fixes it; nothing changes until you press its button.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sbx/default.conf
Settings for every program.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sbx/apps/NAME.conf
Settings for one program; they override the default.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sbx/origins/NAME
The file that runs under NAME. Line 1 is its path, line 2 its size, modification time (ns) and SHA\-256 (absent in files from older versions; added at the next run), line 3 where it moved from. A different file under the same name is handled as described under
.BR "MOVED PROGRAMS" .
.TP
.I ~/.config/sbx/pending/NAME
A decision waiting for you: old=, new=, rule= (c or d) and reason=. Made by sbx, removed by
.B sbx rebind
or when it no longer applies.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sbx/ids/NAME
The machine\-id of NAME.
.TP
.I ~/.sbx\-homes/NAME
The private home of NAME.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sbx/sbx.log
The launch log.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/applications/org.sbx.Gui.desktop
The app menu entry for sbx\-gui, made by
.BR "sbx desktop \-\-install\-menu" .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/applications/sbx\-run.desktop
The "Run in sbx" entry for Open With, made by
.BR "sbx desktop \-\-install\-opener" .
It is hidden from the app menu.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The program's own status, or 128 plus the signal number if a signal ended it. sbx itself exits with 1 when it
cannot start the program (bad option, unreadable path, missing bubblewrap).
.SH LIMITS
32\-bit programs fail because 32\-bit system calls are blocked. Terminal programs have no /dev/tty and no job
control (\fB\-\-new\-session\fR is on), so prompts that read the terminal directly, such as ssh, sudo and git
passwords, do not work. A read\-write share lets the program plant files that later run outside the sandbox (git
hooks, Makefiles, scripts): share only what it needs and prefer read\-only. Each program name has one private
home; use
.B \-\-name
to separate two uses. Programs on another drive (/mnt, /media, /run/media) cannot be started; copy them
elsewhere first. sbx refuses to run as root or inside another sbx sandbox.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bwrap (1),
.BR xdg\-dbus\-proxy (1),
.BR systemd\-run (1),
.BR notify\-send (1)
